Here are safe JavaScript examples for escaping user-provided strings, depending on the context:

1) Escaping for insertion into HTML text nodes:
- Use textContent or a DOM text node to avoid injection.
Example:
const userInput = "<script>alert('billing');</script>";
const div = document.createElement('div');
div.textContent = userInput; // safe: characters rendered as text
document.body.appendChild(div);

2) Escaping for insertion into HTML when building strings:
- Use a small utility to replace special characters with HTML entities.
Example:
function escapeHtml(str) {
  return str
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#39;");
}
const safe = escapeHtml(userInput);
container.innerHTML = safe; // safe because escaped

3) Escaping for use inside JavaScript string contexts:
- Escape user input before embedding in JS strings, or avoid string concatenation by using data attributes or JSON.
Example:
function escapeForJsString(str) {
  return str
    .replace(/\\/g, "\\\\")
    .replace(/'/g, "\\'")
    .replace(/"/g, '\\"')
    .replace(/\n/g, "\\n")
    .replace(/\r/g, "\\r");
}
const jsSafe = escapeForJsString(userInput);
const script = `const data = '${jsSafe}';`;

4) Using libraries:
- For HTML, use DOMPurify to sanitize allowed HTML:
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput);
container.innerHTML = clean;

Notes:
- Prefer setting textContent or using templating libraries that auto-escape (React, Angular, Vue).
- Only use innerHTML with trusted or sanitized content.
- Always apply contextual encoding based on where the data will be used.

